    "latest_minutes_date": "2026-07-29",
    "latest_statement_date": "2026-09-16",
    "minutes": {
      "meeting_date": "2026-07-29",
      "narrative_source": "llm",
      "released_at": "2026-08-19",
      "summary": "At its July 28–29, 2026 meeting, the FOMC voted 9–3 to hold the federal funds rate target range at 3-1/2 to 3-3/4 percent, with three members (Hammack, Kashkari, Logan) preferring a 25 basis point increase. Participants noted the unemployment rate was 4.2 percent in June, largely stable, while economic activity continued expanding at a solid pace, supported by AI-related investment and resilient consumer spending. Total PCE inflation was estimated near 3.7 percent in June, with core PCE near 3.3 percent, both elevated relative to the Committee's 2 percent goal, reflecting tariffs, Middle East conflict-related energy costs, and AI-related demand. Several participants favored raising rates given broad-based price pressures, while most preferred holding steady to gather more information. Participants viewed inflation risks as skewed to the upside and risks to growth and employment as skewed to the downside, citing geopolitical uncertainty and AI-related investment dynamics as key sources of uncertainty.",
      "url": "https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomcminutes20260729.htm"
    }
  },
